### f4c54617e security(agent-coordinator): bound the public faucet with a global budget and per-IP limits

The published `FOLLOWER_API_KEY` legitimately reaches `POST /api/v1/agent/coin-requests/register` and `/execute`, and automatic approval only required a novel `(sender, wallet_address)` pair — both caller-supplied strings that cost nothing to mint. A loop minting fresh pairs collected the 3 AIT ceiling every request; the only bound was the genesis balance.

- `coin_request_policy` now enforces rolling aggregate budgets over `approval_mode='automatic'` rows: `COIN_REQUEST_AUTO_BUDGET_PER_HOUR` (default 24 AIT) and `COIN_REQUEST_AUTO_BUDGET_PER_DAY` (default 60 AIT). Over-cap requests park at `PENDING` for manual review and log a warning for operators; `0` parks everything. The check is an advisory bound — overshoot is bounded by concurrent callers, which the new per-IP limit keeps small.
- `/register` and `/execute` carry per-IP route buckets (30/min, 20/min) via the shared `rate_limit` decorator, keyed on the real client IP (`X-Real-IP`), on top of the service-wide 100/min middleware.
- exchange: `_require_api_key` now uses `hmac.compare_digest` instead of `!=`.
- Tests: budget window/manual-mode/prior-grant interplay, 429s on both routes, and a conftest bucket reset so the process-global limiters cannot leak between files.


### 7da4bde0f feat(agent-coordinator): Prometheus metrics for faucet budget alerting

`decide()` maintains `coin_request_auto_granted_window{window=hourly,daily}` gauges and increments `coin_request_auto_budget_trips_total{window}` each time a request parks on an exhausted budget, so a scrape alert flags an ongoing drain without journal access.


### ops(faucet): post-review verification of f4c54617e — spoofing, dual-hub budget, probe cleanup

Follow-up to the faucet review, all operational (no code change):

- `X-Real-IP` spoofing: both TLS edges (`ns2` fronting hub, `ns3` fronting hub1) set `X-Real-IP $remote_addr` and hub/hub1 nginx only trust their own incus bridge in `set_real_ip_from`. Verified from outside: 34 valid-body `POST /register` calls with 34 distinct forged `X-Real-IP` values → 429 from request 31. The per-IP limit is not bypassable via the header.
- Dual-hub budget: hub1 runs its own agent-coordinator with a separate `coin_requests` DB and holds the genesis key, so its faucet doubled the fleet-wide daily cap to 120 AIT. hub1's `/etc/aitbc/aitbc-agent-coordinator.env` now sets `COIN_REQUEST_AUTO_BUDGET_PER_HOUR=0`; verified a live registration there returns `pending` / "hourly budget is exhausted". Fleet-wide automatic budget is the intended 24/60 AIT again.
- Probe rows: the hub end-to-end test row `budget-probe-0` (1 unit, `automatic/APPROVED`, never executed — `transaction_hash IS NULL`, nothing paid) and the hub1 park-probe row were deleted. Both DBs are back to their pre-review grant totals (hub: 7 automatic approved, 14400000300 units).
- AGENTS.md gains an agent-coordinator faucet section recording the trust chain, the hub1 override, and the probe-row rule.


### 9776248bf fix(agent-coordinator): faucet env knobs are AIT amounts; fleet-check asserts one live faucet

- `COIN_REQUEST_AUTO_BUDGET_PER_HOUR`, `_PER_DAY` and `COIN_REQUEST_AUTO_APPROVE_MAX` were parsed as raw base units while their defaults and AGENTS.md were in AIT — `=24` meant 24 units and parked everything silently. They now parse as decimal AIT via `ait_to_units` (`0` still disables; malformed values fall back to the default with a warning). No live override other than hub1's `=0` existed, so nothing deployed changes meaning.
- `scripts/monitoring/fleet-config-check.sh` gains a faucet section: reads each host's `aitbc-agent-coordinator` EnvironmentFiles and fails unless exactly one host has a non-zero-or-unset hourly budget. Verified from the dev host: hub `<unset>`, hub1 `=0`, node0/1/2 no unit → `ok: exactly one live faucet`. A hub1 reprovision that drops the override now fails the drift check instead of silently doubling the fleet budget.
- Deployed to hub/hub1; live probes (deleted afterwards, unexecuted) confirm hub approves within budget and hub1 parks.

### d6926203b feat(website): env-driven domain + chain-id branding via /etc/aitbc/website.env

The hub site hardcoded its public domain and chain id in `sitemap.xml`, `robots.txt`, `llms.txt`, `structured-data.jsonld`, the `index.html` JSON-LD block, `follower-api-key-announcement.html`, and the `config.js`/`dashboard.js` `chainId`. Since nginx serves the site straight from the checkout, there was no place for per-deployment values.

