### 0adca3b16 fix(fleet-check): host identity, full-reach gate, no abort on one ssh failure; faucet `=inf` guard

- `fleet-config-check.sh` accepted the first ssh candidate that answered as the host. From a fleet node `hub.<domain>` resolves to the `ns2` edge box, which answers ssh — hub's env would have been read from the jump host. Candidates now must match `hostname -s`; a wrong identity prints a NOTE and is ignored (verified from node2: `hub.aitbc.bubuit.net answers as 'ns2', not 'hub' — ignored`).
- Env sections were gated on "any host answers"; now they run only when every host verified, otherwise `NOTE: no verified ssh reach to: <hosts> — env sections SKIPPED` and the convergence section still runs to a clean exit. The `ENVFILES` ssh assignment no longer aborts the run under `set -e`. From node2 the script now completes (env skipped, convergence `No drift`), matching the AGENTS.md "skipped by design" note.
- `coin_request_policy._env_ait` also catches `OverflowError`: `COIN_REQUEST_AUTO_BUDGET_PER_HOUR=inf` was a 500 on every `/register`; it now falls back to the default with a warning. Deployed to hub/hub1.


### d2081dec1 feat(fleet-check): running-process env vs env files for faucet budget and kill-switch

- `fleet-config-check.sh` gains `running_env()`, which reads a variable from `/proc/<MainPID>/environ` of a unit on a host. The faucet section prints `file=… running=…`, counts live faucets by the **running** value, and fails on a mismatch (`edited but not restarted`). The kill-switch check flags `SYNC_VALIDATE_SIGNATURES_SKIP_UNTIL` if set in either files or the running process (case-insensitive, matching pydantic).
- IDE-host run: hub `file=<unset> running=<unset>`, hub1 `file=0 running=0`, `ok: exactly one live faucet`; kill-switch unset everywhere.

### Gossip topology finding (2026-09-27, no change yet — live fix pending operator go)

Ground truth from each node's `Initializing gossip backend:` journal line: **the fleet is hub-and-spoke, not mesh.** hub runs `redis` (local bus only); hub1/node0/node1/node2 run `websocket` to `wss://hub…/rpc/gossip/ws`. The four-peer `GOSSIP_MESH_PEER_URLS` lists still sit in `blockchain.env` on node0/1/2 but `create_backend` only uses them when `GOSSIP_BACKEND=mesh`. hub is again the single point of failure for validator gossip.

Journal backend-transition history (hub and node2 agree): mesh until **2026-09-09 18:11**, then `redis` (same-day restart wave after the F3/F4 remediation — `GOSSIP_BACKEND=redis` in `aitbc-blockchain-node.env`/`-rpc.env` became effective once `node.env` stopped shadowing gossip keys); then `websocket` from **2026-09-26 14:30–14:36** on the followers (hub promotion work wrote the bootstrap `GOSSIP_BACKEND=websocket` + `GOSSIP_WEBSOCKET_URL` pair into their `blockchain.env`). The 2026-09-02 hub-down tests were on mesh; the fleet has not been on mesh since 2026-09-09.

The pre-existing `GOSSIP_BACKEND` drift and shadow CONFLICTs the check reports are this regression. Plan: restore `mesh` + full peer lists one validator at a time with the operator watching, remove the `GOSSIP_BACKEND` lines from unit-specific env files so nothing shadows again, then encode "mesh on every validator, peer list non-empty and excludes self" in the check, then re-run the hub-down test.


### 5c233ac16 fix(gossip): fleet restored to validator mesh after 18-day hub-and-spoke regression

The 2026-09-27 audit found the fleet running hub-and-spoke, not the mesh the repo documents (introduced `3a18de6ea9`, hub-down-tested 2026-09-02). `journalctl` backend history: mesh until 2026-09-09 18:11 (flipped to `redis` by `GOSSIP_BACKEND=redis` lines in the unit env files, exposed when `node.env` stopped shadowing them), then `websocket` from 2026-09-26 (hub promotion wrote the bootstrap spoke pair into followers' `blockchain.env`).

Restored on all five hosts, one at a time, hub first: `blockchain.env` now has `GOSSIP_BACKEND=mesh` + four public `wss://` peer URLs (node0/1/2 keep LAN `ws://` links to each other); the `GOSSIP_BACKEND` lines were removed from `aitbc-blockchain-node.env`/`-rpc.env` (rpc keeps `redis` by design — it is the local-bus bridge); `GOSSIP_WEBSOCKET_URL` and node0's lowercase `gossip_broadcast_url` duplicate are gone. Backups at `/etc/aitbc/*.bak-20260927-*-mesh`.

Verified after each restart: `Initializing gossip backend: mesh` with four peers, established peer connections on hub and node1, fleet converged at equal height/hash, zero gossip warnings.

`fleet-config-check.sh` gains a mesh section asserting the **running** backend (the lesson of this regression — env files lied), a complete self-free peer list, clean shadow lines, rpc-unit `redis`, and case-insensitive greps; `SHADOW_CONFLICT_ALLOW` whitelists the intended `aitbc-blockchain-rpc:GOSSIP_BACKEND` split. A reprovision or drift back to spoke now fails the check.


### convergence probes gain public-endpoint fallback (same commit)

`fleet-config-check.sh` sampled heads via `http://<name>:8202` only — on the
dev host node0/1/2 resolved nowhere and every run ended `DRIFT DETECTED`.
Each host now tries the LAN endpoint first (when `NODE*_HOST` is set) then
`https://<host>.<domain>/rpc/status`. LAN first because a node cannot reach
its own vhost through the public edge (at1 has no hairpin), so `public-only`
