### hub-down gossip test on restored mesh (2026-09-27, ~5 min at 13:55+02:00)

`aitbc-blockchain-node` stopped on hub with the rpc websocket surface left up.

- **Gossip layer: healthy.** hub1/node0/node1/node2 logged zero gossip, mesh,
  peer, or websocket errors for the entire window; the remaining four mesh
  links carried on silently. This is what mesh was built for (`3a18de6ea9`).
- **Block production: halted at 25529.** Only hub produced blocks
  (`0x02B8…E74c5B`). *This conclusion was wrong — corrected in the
  `8aeacd2fd` entry below: proposer rotation exists in `MultiValidatorPoA`,
  but followers had been stripped of production config and hub held all four
  keys.* Followers kept polling
  `/rpc/head` + `/rpc/state/delta` (served by hub's still-running rpc unit,
  200s) and re-verified state roots — "0 accounts changed, state root matches".
- **Recovery: clean.** On restart hub re-initialized `mesh` with 4 peers,
  produced 25530, and all five hosts converged on identical height + hash.
  No orphans, no rejected imports, no state-root divergence. The only warning
  was the proposer's own "6 round(s) without a block" counter noting the
  stall.

Takeaway: mesh removes hub as a gossip single point of failure, as designed.
The production halt shown here was later traced to stripped follower config,
not missing rotation — see the `8aeacd2fd` correction below.


### 8aeacd2fd fix(consensus): four-validator production restored — CORRECTS the earlier hub-down entry

The earlier "hub-down" entry's conclusion was wrong: the PoA layer **does**
rotate proposers (`MultiValidatorPoA`, validated live 2026-09-03). The stall
was a config regression: since the Sep 9–13 env consolidation the followers
ran `ENABLE_BLOCK_PRODUCTION=false` with `VALIDATOR_KEYS={}`, while hub held
all four validator keys and signed every block in every validator's name —
metadata rotated, production didn't. For ~18 days one host's key compromise
would have controlled the chain, and nothing caught it because receivers never
check who signed (PBFT off, `validate_block` has no production callers).

Restored with single-holder sequencing: each validator now holds exactly its
own key (hub `0x02B8`, hub1 `0x9Ea1`, node1 `0x241D`, node2 `0x4364`), hub's
extra maps removed (rpc holds none), `PROPOSER_ID` corrected per host,
`ENABLE_BLOCK_PRODUCTION=true` re-armed on hub1/node1/node2, and
`CONSENSUS_PROPOSER_ROUND_SECONDS` unset so the window derives to 120 s (it
had been set to 60 = the heartbeat, which would have made every block land in
a foreign round once keys were split).

Re-tested with **both** hub units down for ~9 min (14:18–14:27 UTC):
**failover verified.** Rotation order is the address-sorted active set
`[hub, node1, node2, hub1]` and the round window derives to
`max(30, heartbeat)` = 60 s (unset). With hub fully down, three hub-owned
slots were produced by live validators: height 25652 — hub's round-0 slot —
by node1 at round 1; 25656 — also hub's round-0 — by node1 at round 1; and
25651 + 25655 — hub's round-1 slots after hub1 missed round 0 — by node1 at
round 2. The chain never stalled; blocks landed every ~60–126 s. Note the
schedule quirk this exposed: the heartbeat gate fires at ≥60 s idle, exactly
when round 0 ends, so round-0 owners never produce *empty* blocks — every
heartbeat lands at round ≥1 by the next validator. Consistent everywhere, so
not a fork vector, but slot-0 identity never matches the signer for empty
blocks.

Hub rejoin verified the fix below: production disabled via runtime drop-in,
bulk pull from hub1 caught up 10 heights automatically, head matched at
25657, drop-in removed, second restart resumed production — no fork. Anyone
auditing "four independent validators" for the Sep 13 – Sep 27 window should
know they weren't.

**New defect found by the earlier test:** a node that restarts on a stale
head forks instead of catching up — hub proposed competing blocks 25558–60
(its sync source was itself: `DEFAULT_PEER_RPC_URL` empty, so the sync gate
never trips; imports reject gaps; `_resolve_fork` detects but never reorgs),
and node0 pulled the fork through hub's rpc before it was stopped. Repaired
manually (fork rows deleted — all empty heartbeats — then `sync_cli` bulk
pull from node1). Mitigations now in place and proven: hub syncs from hub1
via `CHAIN_SYNC_SOURCES`, AGENTS.md has a mandatory production-disabled
rejoin runbook for every validator restart/deploy (exercised live here), and
post-repair state audit shows identical `state_root`, account count (58),
and full account-table hash across all five hosts. Code fixes (pre-proposal
freshness check, deterministic fork choice, receiver-side `validate_block`)
remain open.

`fleet-config-check.sh` now asserts the running backend is `mesh` everywhere
with complete self-free peer lists, and a new validator section enforces:
exactly one own-derived key per validator present in `VALIDATOR_SET`, no
address held by two hosts, production on for validators and off for node0, and
no explicit round window. The Sep 9–13 regression would now fail the check.

### 454dcfa7a feat(consensus): pre-proposal freshness gate — stale-head rejoin fixed

The fork above is now closed in code. Before assembling a block, every node
fetches `/rpc/head` from all `GOSSIP_MESH_PEER_URLS` peers in parallel
(`wss://…/rpc/gossip/ws` → `https://` origin, 2 s per-peer timeout):

- peer ahead → skip the proposal and bulk-pull **from that peer** via the new
  `SyncManager.pull_from_peer` — catch-up no longer depends on the single
  `CHAIN_SYNC_SOURCES` star;
- same height, different hash → skip and count
  `proposal_freshness_hash_mismatch_total` (deterministic fork choice is the
  next task; proposing on top would only deepen the fork);
- all peers unreachable → propose anyway and count
  `proposal_freshness_unverified_total` — a partition must not halt the chain;
- verdicts cached per local head; the gate never touches the network when
  `ENABLE_BLOCK_PRODUCTION=false`, which the rejoin runbook relies on.

**Live verification:** hub node+rpc stopped for ~4 min (4+ round windows,
network advanced to 25684), then started with production ON — no drop-in.
It bulk-pulled from hub1, reached the fleet head, produced nothing while
stale, and no fork formed. Fleet converged at 25687. The runbook stays as
defence in depth; the gate alone cannot resolve a same-height hash split —
that is the fork-choice task.

Two facts the earlier analysis got wrong, corrected in AGENTS.md: the round
window derives to `max(30, heartbeat)` = 60 s (not 120), and the rotation
order is the **address-sorted** active set `[hub, node1, node2, hub1]`, not
`VALIDATOR_SET` order. Empty blocks are always produced at round ≥1 by the
next validator — the intended price of the heartbeat-aligned round from the
C-2 exercise — so the chain stays live with any single validator up, but a
partition leaves both sides producing, which is why fork choice is next.

### b1c649ce9 feat(consensus): freshness gate hardening — vote, quarantine, cache TTL

Review of `454dcfa7a` found three ways the first version could either halt
the fleet or re-open the fork it closed; all fixed:

- **Hash votes, not vetoes.** One divergent peer previously produced
  HASH_MISMATCH for everyone → nobody proposed → chain halt. Now the local
  hash votes alongside peers': a rival hash only holds a proposal when it
  has STRICTLY more support (forked minority holds, majority proceeds); a
  tie proceeds and bumps `proposal_freshness_hash_tie_total` — holding on a
  tie would be the same halt.
- **Stale pulls retry; useless peers quarantine.** A cached STALE verdict
  previously lasted forever if the pull did not move the head (taller fork
  the importer rejects, peer overstating). Verdicts now expire after
  `PROPOSAL_FRESHNESS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS` (5 s, ≪ the 60 s round window), the
  pull is re-kicked on every fresh check (SyncManager dedupes), and the gate
  records `peer → local height` at kick time: when `pull_in_flight` clears
  and the head did not move, the peer is quarantined for
  `PROPOSAL_FRESHNESS_PEER_QUARANTINE_SECONDS` (600 s ≈ 10 windows) and
  excluded from the ahead check only — it still votes on hashes. Every ahead
  peer quarantined → proceed + `proposal_freshness_peer_quarantined_total`
  (markings counted by `proposal_freshness_peer_quarantine_marked_total`).
- **UNVERIFIED is never cached.** A network blip previously cached "OK" per
  local head, so a stale validator could propose right when connectivity
  returned. UNVERIFIED is re-checked every tick; FRESH/STALE/HASH_MISMATCH
  expire with the TTL so a verdict cannot survive into a later round.
- **Malformed peer heads count as unreachable.** `/rpc/head` replies with
  non-int `height` or non-str `hash` now return None inside `_fetch_head`
  instead of raising into the proposer loop. Injected results are
  re-validated in `check()` too.

28 freshness tests + 136 consensus tests pass; ruff/mypy clean.

### 8b82864c9 feat(sync): deterministic fork choice — stale forks now heal themselves

`_resolve_fork` finally resolves: a same-height block conflict is decided by
`(proposer round, block hash)` — both derivable from headers alone, so every
node picks the same winner, including the validator that produced the losing
block. Two decision points:

- **push path** (`import_block` → `_resolve_fork`): when the rival block
  shares our block's parent, compare rounds via the shared parent timestamp.
  Rival wins → reorg: delete our blocks `>=` the contested height and append
  the rival — **but only when every removed block is provably empty**
  (`tx_count == 0` and `state_root ==` its parent's). There is no state-undo
  path; a non-empty losing segment still escalates as an operator divergence
  rather than corrupting state. Different parent → undecidable inline, stays
  diverged for the pull resolver.
- **pull path** (`bulk_import_from` diverged → `_resolve_fork_with_peer`):
  walks back from our head fetching the peer's block per height until hashes
  match (bounded by `max_reorg_depth`), compares the two children of the
  common ancestor by the same key, and when the peer's branch wins + the
  losing segment is empty, deletes it and restarts the pull at the fork
  point. When ours wins the peer is on the losing branch and gets the
  divergence report instead.

This is the exact repair that needed manual SQL on 2026-09-27: canonical
blocks now evict fork blocks deterministically on pull or push, so a healed
validator's followers re-converge without operator surgery. The freshness
gate's proceed-on-tie rule is what this makes safe: tied votes produce
competing schedule-valid blocks, and fork choice settles them.

Metrics: `sync_fork_choice_compared_total`, `..._local_wins_total`,
`..._remote_wins_total`, `sync_fork_reorg_unsafe_total`, `sync_reorgs_total`,
`sync_reorg_depth`.

10 fork-choice tests + 241 sync/consensus tests pass; ruff/mypy clean.

