Verified: `nginx -t`+reload on all touched hosts; `/rpc/subscribe/ws` → 101
on all five public names (a coincidental node2 rpc service restart mid-test
briefly showed 502 — unrelated, recovered); hub1 `/agent/bootstrap.env`
200, `genesis.json` 200, `blockchain.env`/`chain.db`/`secrets.env` → 404,
`messages/stream` → 401 (auth parity with hub).

- **Full-tie fork deferral (commits 26add79f4, 9d61f2b1d3).** The first
  live fork proof at height 25841 was decided on *segment length*
  (proposers 1 vs 1, length 2 vs 1) — hub1 legitimately authored both
  majority blocks, so the distinct-proposer signal never actually
  differentiated. Had the partition ended one block earlier the segments
  would have tied 1-vs-1 on both counts and `(round, hash)` would have
  wrongly preferred the isolated node's round-1 block. Two changes close
  that: (a) a full proposer+length tie now *defers* the reorg for one
  proposer-round window instead of key-breaking immediately — the
  isolated side's freshness gate keeps it from proposing while the
  majority keeps building, so the next comparison decides by length —
  and only a tie that survives the window (a genuinely symmetric split)
  falls to `(round, hash)`; (b) equal-height head divergence now engages
  the pull resolver at all (it was previously detected and discarded by
  the sync manager, so the resolver only ever ran once the peer was
  strictly ahead — when its segment was already longer). Demonstrated
  live at height 25937 during a scripted partition of node2
  (`scripts/multi-node/partition-fork-test.sh`): the first post-restore
  check logged `Fork tie at height 25937 (proposers 1 vs 1, length 1 vs
  1) — deferring reorg for one 60s round window`, held node2's local
  block, and 62s later — after the majority produced 25938 — resolved
  `peer branch wins … length 2 vs 1` and reorged atomically. All five
  nodes converged at 25939 on the majority chain.
- **RPC near-head cache bypass (commit 26add79f4).** Complementing the
  header-cache TTL: `/rpc/block/{height}` now skips `BlockHeaderCache`
  entirely for heights within `max_reorg_depth` of the current head —
  the only heights a reorg can still rewrite — so a just-orphaned header
  is never served during the TTL window either. `/rpc/blocks-range`
  (peer sync) was confirmed to read the DB directly and was unaffected.

- **Consensus hardening batch (2026-09-28).** The follow-up to the fork
  work above:
  - **Attester lock (`bca5ae618b`).** A validator that signs a peer's
    block X at height h records `(h, X)` in-memory and, for one proposer
    round, refuses to attest a rival hash at h and suppresses its own
    proposal there — it fetches X from the mesh and imports it instead.
    Removes the equivocation that let an attester who never received the
    block it signed build a competing one (`ATTESTATION_LOCK_ENABLED=false`
    restores legacy behaviour; locks are in-memory and reset on restart).
  - **Delta-journal undo (`ce49bf601c`).** Non-empty losing fork segments
    no longer require a manual snapshot reset: every block apply journals
    per-row before-images (`block_state_delta` — ORM writes plus the raw
    `UPDATE account` balance/nonce writes) inside the block's own
    commit, fork resolution replays them in reverse, and the restore is
    proven by recomputing the state root against the common ancestor's
    recorded root before commit. Orphaned transaction payloads are
    requeued into the mempool (best-effort: the stored row does not carry
    the original signature, so a payload that fails re-admission is
    dropped — the winning branch normally carries it anyway). Missing
    journal or root mismatch still escalates via
    `sync_fork_reorg_unsafe_total` — fail-closed, never a wrong revert.
    New metric `sync_fork_reorg_undone_total`.
  - **Chain-ID signature binding (`8b61bed96a`).** Tests pin that block
    and attestation signatures cover `chain_id` — a signature from the
    retired `ait-hub1.aitbc.bubuit.net` chain cannot replay on
    `ait-hub.aitbc.bubuit.net` or vice versa. The stale `ait-hub1` data
    directory on hub1 (zero blocks, untouched since Sep 25) was renamed
    aside; no env anywhere still references that chain.
  - **Websocket publish scope (`0fe3e606b8`).** Tests pin the `05369d6262`
    fix: a `gossip_broker.publish` failure drops and counts the message
    (`gossip_broker_publish_failed_total`) without closing the connection,
    while authentication and oversized-message failures still close it.
  - **Alerting (`a6307d06d0`).** `ForkReorgUnsafe` and
    `FaucetBudgetTripped` rules added to `scripts/monitoring/aitbc_rules.yml`
    (13 rules). Note: the coordinator scrape job in node2's
    prometheus.yml is still commented out, so the faucet rule stays inert
    until that endpoint is scraped.
  - **Docs (`6413d8fd4c`).** AGENTS.md liveness text corrected to the real
    model: proposer + 2 remote attestations (3-of-4); one validator down
    tolerated, two down or a symmetric 2–2 split halts production; forks
    need an asymmetric failure.
  - **Cut-7 transaction verified canonical.** The `GPU_MARKET`
    `software_offer` from node2's orphaned 25885' (`0x8c29df4f…`)
    is present in the canonical `0xaeacc3a8` block at 25885 on hub1 and
    node2 — the majority mempool held it pre-partition. No resubmission
    needed.
  - **Delta-journal hardening (`7746c02fc3`).** Non-empty fork-segment
    undo is now fail-closed: an unresolved insert pk, unrecognised DML on a
    chain table (Core update/delete, query().update, bulk_*, raw SQL beyond
    the account-PK form, INSERT OR IGNORE/REPLACE upserts), or a mid-apply
    transaction boundary stamps an `incomplete` sentinel and
    `revert_losing_segment` refuses the block — the resolver escalates
    instead of partially reverting. `Transaction` gained an `envelope`
    column (auto-migrated) carrying the full signed transaction, so orphaned
    transactions requeue through real mempool admission; the sync export/
    import path preserves it. Orphans that fail requeue and are absent from
    the winning branch log a WARNING and count
    `sync_fork_orphaned_tx_lost_total` — the new `ForkOrphanedTxLost` alert
    (critical, same path as ForkReorgUnsafe). Delta pruning now runs every
    64th persist. Differential tests
    (`tests/test_block_deltas_differential.py`, 13 cases) snapshot every
    chain table around apply+revert for TRANSFER, GPU_MARKET variants,
    GPU_REGISTER, ESCROW lock/release/refund, BRIDGE lock/release/refund,
    BOND lock/release/slash and GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE — byte-identical restore
    in all families. `sync_fork_undo_enabled` (env SYNC_FORK_UNDO_ENABLED)
    gates non-empty undo, default on. AGENTS.md documents that the
    delta-sync fast path is not journaled and fails closed on later undo.
  - **Coordinator metrics exposition fix (`ffd7eedaef`).** The
    agent-coordinator `/v1/metrics` endpoint emitted unquoted label values
    (`method=GET`), which is invalid Prometheus text — every scrape errored,
    which is why the FaucetBudgetTripped rule was inert. Label values are
    now quoted/escaped. Hub's Prometheus scrapes
    `localhost:8107/v1/metrics` and evaluates the full ruleset (all five
    nodes' rules files synced — 14 rules including ForkOrphanedTxLost).
  - **Side-table revert digest + domain-row orphaning (df513eea4f).** Each
    journaled block now stores a __digest__ meta row: a SHA-256 of every
    non-account chain table it touched, taken pre-apply. Revert re-hashes
    each digested table and fails closed on mismatch — the revert proof now
    covers escrow/bridge/bond/etc., not just balances via the state root.
    The digest caught a real capture gap on first run: a row mutated across
    two flushes kept only the first upd's before-image; repeat upds now
    merge before-images per attribute (earliest wins). RPC queue-time rows
    (stake, agent_stake, bounty_contract) are unreachable by the journal;
    when their confirming STAKE_LOCK/BOUNTY_LOCK is lost in a reorg the row
    is marked status='orphaned' and counted via
    sync_fork_domain_rows_orphaned_total, unless another confirmed lock
    still references it. Differential coverage extended to all apply-able
    families (26 cases). Pre-existing stale test
    test_get_block_version_from_metadata updated for v8 advisory-stamp
    semantics (ad551fcefa). Dead commented scrape samples removed from the
    prometheus.yml template and node2's live file (f612920426).
  - **Requeue hardening + post-import reconcile + revert checks
    (325fc731a8, 6b0a538bc4).** Alert-path counters are pre-registered at
    zero at process start (blockchain node: all sync_fork_* / reorg /
    divergence counters; coordinator: both coin_request_auto_budget_trips
    windows) so the first event produces a real increase() delta in
    Prometheus. Orphaned-transaction requeue now runs through full public
    admission — signature verification plus _validate_transaction_admission
    (chain id, sender existence, balance + pending-cost, nonce window) —
    instead of raw mempool.add; a tx that fails and is absent on the
    winning branch is counted lost (legacy/unsigned reconstructions are
    never admitted). PREREGISTERED_CREDIT_TX_TYPES stay exempt from
    signature checks but keep chain/balance checks. Domain-row orphan
    marking is deferred until after the winning branch is imported: the
    pull resolver collects failed requeue candidates, imports the winner,
    then reconcile_orphaned_domain_rows() runs _tx_on_chain /
    confirmed_lock_txs against the post-import state — a lock on the
    winning branch no longer false-orphans a stake/agent_stake/bounty row.
    Reconciliation is idempotent and un-orphans a row when a surviving
    lock reappears. Digest-cap skips (100k rows) now mark the journal
    incomplete instead of warn-and-continue. Delete before-images use
    attribute history (history.deleted / loaded committed values), fixing
    the update-then-delete-in-one-flush case. revert_losing_segment adds a
    structural post-check: zero losing-segment transaction/block rows may
    remain, else it fails closed. New alerts: ForkReorgUndone (a reorg
    undid journaled state) and ForkDomainRowsOrphaned. New tests cover
    composite blocks (multi-tx same sender, create-and-spend,
    escrow lock+release in one block), update-then-delete, digest-cap
    fail-closed, digest tamper, signed-envelope admission, unsigned ->
    lost, and orphan/un-orphan reconciliation. Live canary on hub:
    TRANSFER 0x62b895… confirmed at 26722 and GPU_REGISTER 0xb01270… at
    26724 — envelopes persisted on both rows (527/679 bytes),
    gpu_registration side-table row created, __digest__ meta row carried
    a real pre-state hash for gpu_registration; fleet converged
    hash-identical. Note: poa.py proposer path rewrites
    tx_data_for_transition['nonce'] = sender_account.nonce before apply —
    the signed nonce is ignored at execution; flagged as a latent
    consensus divergence risk (a follower validates the signed nonce,
    the proposer executes the current one) — not changed, needs a
    decision.

